---

<PaperLayout
  title="Unleash the Potential of CLIP"
  subtitle="for Video Highlight Detection"
  venue="CVPR 2024 Workshop · Efficient Large Vision Models"
  authors={authors}
  links={links}
  description="HL-CLIP: state-of-the-art video highlight detection by fine-tuning CLIP's last layers and applying saliency pooling — no SlowFast, no DETR."
>
  <section>
    <h2>Abstract</h2>
    <p>
      Multimodal pre-trained models have unlocked open-world knowledge across many
      tasks; the video domain is no exception. We present <strong>Highlight-CLIP
      (HL-CLIP)</strong>, a method designed to excel at video highlight detection by
      leveraging the pre-trained knowledge embedded in multimodal encoders. Simply
      fine-tuning the multimodal encoder together with our <em>saliency pooling</em>
      technique achieves state-of-the-art on the QVHighlights benchmark.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>TL;DR</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Detector-free.</strong> No DETR head, no SlowFast features. Just CLIP
        with a focused fine-tuning strategy and a lightweight inference-time pooling.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Tune the last two layers.</strong> Only the final two layers of CLIP's
        vision and text encoders are fine-tuned — preserving pre-trained semantics.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Saliency pooling.</strong> At inference, average predicted saliency
        across neighboring frames. Robust to per-frame noise without retraining.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>SOTA on QVHighlights.</strong> 42.40 mAP / 72.42 HIT@1 on val;
        41.94 / 70.60 on test — exceeding CG-DETR (40.33 mAP).
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Method</h2>
    <figure>
      <img src="/hlclip/img/arch.png" alt="HL-CLIP architecture: dual-encoder fine-tuning with saliency pooling" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>HL-CLIP architecture. Frame embeddings from a fine-tuned CLIP vision encoder are scored against the query embedding via cosine similarity; saliency pooling averages predictions across neighboring frames at inference.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3>Training</h3>
    <p>
      We stack <em>K</em>×<em>N</em> frame–query pairs and supervise with MSE between
      predicted and ground-truth saliency. Only the last two encoder layers in CLIP
      receive gradient updates, which preserves the broad pre-trained semantics that
      make CLIP useful in the first place while specializing the upper layers for the
      saliency task.
    </p>

    <h3>Saliency pooling</h3>
    <p>
      At inference, we average predicted saliency over a small temporal window
      around each frame. This trades off marginal localization sharpness for a
      meaningful gain in robustness — converting per-frame noise into temporally
      smoothed scores that better match human-rated highlights.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Main results — QVHighlights</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="results">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Method</th>
            <th>Val mAP</th>
            <th>Val HIT@1</th>
            <th>Test mAP</th>
            <th>Test HIT@1</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="method">CG-DETR (prior SOTA)</td>
            <td>40.33</td><td>—</td><td>40.33</td><td>—</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="divider">
            <td class="method">HL-CLIP-2</td>
            <td>40.00</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>—</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="highlight">
            <td class="method">HL-CLIP-2 + Saliency Pooling</td>
            <td>42.40</td><td>72.42</td><td>41.94</td><td>70.60</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="table-caption">Saliency pooling alone contributes +2.40 mAP at validation — the design choice that turned the approach into SOTA.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Limitation</h2>
    <p>
      HL-CLIP is detector-free by design. It identifies salient moments without
      localizing precise start/end times — i.e., it solves highlight detection but
      not full moment retrieval. Combining HL-CLIP scores with a lightweight
      span head is a natural extension.
    </p>
  </section>

